  "source": "icanspeak Italian original text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-11-12)",
  "subtitle": "From 0 to 2000. Italian writes numbers as one word: diciassette, ventidue, centoventi. Before uno and otto the tens lose their last vowel: ventuno, trentotto. Before a noun, uno works like the article: un euro, una casa. Euro never changes: due euro.",
  "briefMarks": ["ventuno", "ventotto", "ventitré", "cento", "mille", "duemila", "euro"],
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "11–19: one word", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "20–99: tens + ones, one word", "variant": "verb" },
      { "text": "100 cento · 1000 mille · 2000 duemila" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "16 → sedici · 22 → ventidue · 23 → ventitré · 28 → ventotto · 31 → trentuno · 45 → quarantacinque · 100 → cento · 150 → centocinquanta · 2000 → duemila",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>11 to 16</strong> end in <em>-dici</em>: <em>undici, dodici, tredici, quattordici, quindici, sedici</em>. <strong>17 to 19</strong> start with <em>dicia-</em>: <em>diciassette, diciotto, diciannove</em>.",
        "tag": "11–19"
      },
      {
        "html": "From <strong>20</strong>, put the tens and the ones together in <strong>one word</strong>: <em>ventidue, trentacinque, novantanove</em>. Before <em>uno</em> and <em>otto</em> the tens drop their last vowel: <em>ventuno, ventotto, trentuno, quarantotto</em>.",
        "tag": "20–99"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>tre</strong> at the end of a longer number takes an accent: <em>ventitré, trentatré, centotré</em>. On its own: <em>tre</em>.",
        "tag": "-tré"
      },
      {
        "html": "Before a noun, <strong>uno</strong> changes like the article: <em>un euro, uno studente, una casa, un'ora</em>. On its own it stays <em>uno</em>: <em>Quanti figli hai? – Uno.</em> With <em>ventuno, trentuno</em> the noun is plural: <em>ventuno persone</em>.",
        "tag": "uno, un, una"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>cento</strong> never changes: <em>duecento, cinquecento</em>. <strong>mille</strong> becomes <strong>mila</strong> after 1000: <em>duemila, tremila</em>. Still one word: <em>milleduecento, duemilaventisei</em>.",
        "tag": "cento, mille"
      },
      {
        "html": "In Italy a comma marks decimals and a point marks thousands: <em>12,50 €</em> is <em>dodici euro e cinquanta</em> or just <em>dodici e cinquanta</em>. <em>1.250 €</em> is <em>milleduecentocinquanta euro</em>. <strong>euro</strong> never changes: <em>un euro, due euro</em>.",
        "tag": "Prices"
      },
      {
        "html": "Phone numbers: mobile numbers start with 3, landlines with 0 (<em>06</em> for Rome). People say them digit by digit or in small groups: <em>347 12 34 567</em> → <em>tre quattro sette, dodici, trentaquattro, cinque sei sette</em>.",
        "tag": "Phone numbers"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>ventiuno</em> → ✓ <em>ventuno</em>. ✗ <em>due euri</em> → ✓ <em>due euro</em>. ✗ <em>duemille</em> → ✓ <em>duemila</em>. ✗ <em>ventitre</em> → ✓ <em>ventitré</em>. ✗ <em>Sono 42 anni</em> → ✓ <em>Ho quarantadue anni</em>: age goes with <a href=\"/stem-changing-verbs\">avere</a>. Use numbers with <a href=\"/time-and-days\">the time</a>.",
